WEBVTT

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:11.343
Cursor’s cloud agents can pick up tickets too. The agent reads the ticket, works in the product’s repository, and opens a pull request, and the link lands on the ticket.

00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:17.895
In Apps, under Developer tools, click Connect on Cursor. There are two steps in Cursor first.

00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:29.811
Create an API key in your Cursor dashboard. And in Cursor, connect GitHub, for the repositories its agents may change. Cursor works in your code through its own GitHub connection.

00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:40.144
Then continue, and paste the key. Worklynq checks it, and loads the repositories your Cursor account can reach. Agent usage is billed to that Cursor account.

00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:49.795
Once it’s connected, Cursor shows the repositories it can reach. Map each product to its repository, so its tickets go to the right code.

00:00:50.233 --> 00:00:58.873
On the Cursor tile, you can also set a maximum run time. Cursor has no cost cap per session, so keep an eye on your Cursor plan’s usage.

00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:10.427
Cursor starts from a workflow. Add the Cursor agent action to a stage of a ticket type, and every ticket that reaches that stage starts an agent, with the instructions you choose.

00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:19.208
The ticket shows the agent’s status and summary, and the pull request it opened, for your team to review. Your team decides what gets merged.

00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:28.712
Like Claude and GitHub, turn the Cursor section on for each ticket type first, under Ticket Types, Sections. While it’s off, that type can’t start an agent.

